```rust
fn lexer(input: &[u8]) -> IResult<&[u8], Vec<(&[u8], usize)>> {
    map_res(alt((tag("".""), tag(""/""))), |t| (t, 1))(input)
}
```
This function splits the input into two categories: `.` and `/`, each one is tagged with its size of 1 for simplicity. In a real-world application, you would probably want to handle more complex patterns.

2. The `parse_grammar` function:

```rust
fn parse_grammar(input: &[u8]) -> IResult<&[u8], Vec<GrammarNode>> {
    map_res(lexer, |t| match t {
        (b""."", 1) => Ok(GrammarNode::LiteralString(vec![b'.'])),
        (b""/"", 1) => Ok(GrammarNode::Identifier(vec![b'/'])),
        _ => Err(GrammarError::ParseFailure),
    })(input)
}
```
This function checks the tag and size of each byte sequence produced by `lexer`. If it matches ""."" or ""/"", it produces a corresponding `GrammarNode`, otherwise it returns an error.

